Falsifies `paging::sv39::verification::the_leaf_keeps_address_and_permissions_apart`.

The defect: `PPN_SHIFT` moved from 10 to 12, so `leaf_entry` writes the physical page
number two bits above where Sv39 puts it and `entry_pa` reads it back from the same wrong
place. Every leaf the kernel installs then names a physical page four times too high, and
two of the PTE's permission bits are overwritten by address bits. This is one constant,
which is what makes it the plausible version of the defect.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(((leaf >> 10) & ((1 << 44) - 1)) << 12, pa)`, on any
non-zero physical address.

**Measured blind before milestone 211.** The harness used to assert
`Sv39::entry_pa(Sv39::leaf_entry(pa, flags)) == pa`, which is an encoder round-tripped
through its own decoder: the two move together and the round trip holds for every
consistently wrong pair. Confirmed by applying this diff against the old phrasing and
watching it stay green. The rewrite states the bit positions the RISC-V privileged
specification assigns, as literals rather than through this crate's own `PPN_SHIFT`,
because a harness that cited the constant would move with it. `aarch64.rs` and `x86_64.rs`
carry the same rewrite for the same reason; this crate's `no_vtd_entry_ever_sets_a_reserved_bit`
was already written this way.

